@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the European folk-magic transmission
#
# Opened from the register because of what its handbooks carry and almost
# nothing else in this graph does: the conditions under which a procedure is
# said to be spoiled, stated as time, silence and material, and in one place a
# count of applications with the failures kept in it. The tradition instance
# was registered in traditions/registry-western-esoteric.ttl and is moved here
# now that passages have been read.
#
# What the file does not do is settle the reading of any charm. It holds the
# conditions, because the conditions are the part that can fail.
#

pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "European folk magic"@en, "Europäische Volksmagie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of household charms, blessings and remedies in handbooks passed from hand to hand, manuscript before it was printed, in which the words are given together with the conditions of time, silence and material under which they are to be used."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the first time in two printings of one handbook and in the booklet that stands behind it: Hohman's Der lange verborgene Freund in the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, its Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty, and the Romanus-Büchlein first dated to Glatz seventeen eighty-eight. The reading was directed by term through the full text of both Hohman printings and not carried page by page; the pharmacological recipes, which state no condition and claim no capacity, were passed over. The remaining works named in the principal corpus were not opened."@en ;
    skos:note "Read a second time on the thirteenth of September twenty twenty-six, in three printings rather than two: the Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight was opened beside the Reading original and the Harrisburg translation. That third printing settles a question the first reading left open, because the appendix that carries the transfer of a disease to a cow stands there and not in the original, and the Reading printing of eighteen twenty has no appendix at all. The reading of this run was directed at the front matter, at the closing pages and at the appendix, and the middle of the book was again searched by term and not carried page by page."@en ;
    skos:note "The definition was widened in this run from printed handbooks to handbooks, manuscript before printed, and the Old English Herbarium was added to the principal corpus. The reason is that pm:principalCorpus already named Lacnunga and Bald's Leechbook, which are manuscript and not printed, so the definition was narrower than the corpus it stood over; the Herbarium travels in the same edition and the same medical transmission. The widening is a decision and is named here rather than made silently."@en ;
    skos:note "The two Hohman printings are held apart deliberately. They differ at two places where the difference falls on the examinable part of a claim, and both differences stand in this file as their own nodes rather than being smoothed into one reading."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Merseburg charms; Lacnunga; Bald's Leechbook; the Old English Herbarium of pseudo-Apuleius; Romanusbüchlein; Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis; Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa.: gedruckt für den Verfasser, 1820)" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), the literal English translation of the preceding" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa.: gedruckt bey J. B., 1828), the third printing, opened for its appendix" ;
    dcterms:source "Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864)" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unbeschrauen"@en, "Unbeschrauen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition, attached to a procedure and not to a formula, that the act be carried out without being called at and without the doing being spoken of. It is set on cutting a sod, on knotting twigs, on hanging a written letter around the neck, on passing water into a vessel and on going to a wagon, that is on acts of every kind, and never on the words themselves."@en ;
    skos:note "The eighteen fifty translation renders the term as unbeshrewedly, a coinage with no currency in English, which is why the identifier keeps the German word of the printing that carries it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 14, 27, 28, 29, 53, 83" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The window of weekday and moon"@en, "Das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition that an act fall on a named weekday, before sunrise, and in a named quarter of the moon: Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the written slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism, Thursday morning before sunrise and in the open air for the binding of a thief."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27, 29, 53" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32, 41" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The knotting of three twigs against the fits"@en, "Das Knüpfen dreier Schüsse gegen die Gichter"@de ;
    skos:definition "Going onto another man's land, saying that one goes before another court and knots one's seventy-seven kinds of fits, cutting three shoots there and making a knot in each, on a Friday before sunrise in the waning moon and unbeschrauen, then making the crosses over the body at the place where the fits are felt."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ceasing of the fits"@en, "Das Weichen der Gichter"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effect the knotting is performed for: that the fits, counted by the charm as seventy-sevenfold, leave the body at the place the crosses are made."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The knotting drives out the fits"@en, "Das Knüpfen vertreibt die Gichter"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesUnbeschrauen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The knotting presupposes that it is done unbeschrauen"@en, "Das Knüpfen setzt voraus, dass es unbeschrauen geschieht"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, because the passage gives the condition as part of the instruction and says nothing about what follows if it is broken. The book has consequences elsewhere and none here, and the difference is kept rather than filled in."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesMondfenster a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The knotting presupposes the window of weekday and moon"@en, "Das Knüpfen setzt das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The sewn letter against rheumatism"@en, "Der eingenähte Brief gegen das Reissen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A written letter sewn into a piece of cloth and hung around the neck, where cloth, tape and thread must never have been in water, the thread must carry no knot, and cloth and tape must have been spun by a child of not more than seven years; the hanging on is unbeschrauen and falls in a named Friday of the moon, and the Lord's prayer and the articles of faith are said immediately after."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBriefFrist a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Which Friday of the moon the letter is hung on"@en, "An welchem Freitag des Mondes der Brief angehängt wird"@de ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The eighteen twenty printing sets the hanging on at the last Friday in the old light, that is at the end of the waning moon. The eighteen fifty translation sets it at the first Friday in the decreasing moon, which is the other end of the same quarter. The two cannot both be followed, and the difference falls on the only part of the prescription that could be missed."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would require the intervening American printings, which were not opened; that the translation is called literal on its own title page is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either side."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Blue vitriol in the hollow tooth"@en, "Blauer Vitriol im hohlen Zahn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Putting a small piece of blue vitriol into the hollow tooth when the pain begins, not too much, and spitting out the water that collects rather than swallowing it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The driving out of the severest toothache"@en, "Das Vertreiben der allerheftigsten Zahnschmerzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effect claimed for the vitriol, stated for hollow teeth and left open by the author for teeth that are not hollow."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The vitriol drives out the toothache"@en, "Der Vitriol vertreibt die Zahnschmerzen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The rare part of this claim is not the remedy but the number attached to it. The author states more than sixty applications and says that on one of them it did not help. A transmission that keeps its own failure in the count is uncommon in this graph, and it is what makes the claim examinable rather than merely asserted; what the number does not carry is a base rate, since toothache remits without treatment."@en ;
    skos:note "The author also states outright that he does not know whether the remedy works on teeth that are not hollow. The limit is his and is kept here rather than widened."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolZaehlungStrittig a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "On whom the sixty applications were made"@en, "An wem die sechzig Anwendungen geschahen"@de ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The eighteen twenty printing says the author drove away his own severest toothaches more than sixty times. The eighteen fifty translation says he has cured the severest toothache more than sixty times and names no patient, which reads as a series over other people. The number is the same and the population it runs over is not, and a count whose population is unsettled cannot be compared with anything."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The three chips in the wheel hub"@en, "Die drey Spänlein in der Rad-Nabe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cutting three chips at the place the thief went out, going to a wagon unbeschrauen, taking off a wheel, putting the chips into the hub and driving the wheel backwards while the compelling words are spoken."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 83-84" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 52-53" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRadKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Do not drive the wheel too hard"@en, "Treib das Rad nicht gar zu stark um"@de ;
    skos:definition "The warning against turning the wheel too hard, printed inside the spoken charm and addressed to the operator rather than to the thief the surrounding words address."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The place of the sentence is the finding. A caution set inside the words to be spoken cannot be read aloud without being obeyed or noticed, and an operator working from the page meets it at the moment it applies. What it does not say is what turning too hard would do."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 84" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 53" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The bent juniper bush"@en, "Der gebogene Wachholderbusch"@de ;
    skos:definition "Going before sunrise to a juniper bush, bending it towards the rising sun with the left hand while telling it that it will be bent and pressed until the thief has carried the stolen goods back, then laying a stone on the bush and the skull of a malefactor beneath stone and bush."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 65, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Loose the bush again once the goods are back"@en, "Den Busch wieder losmachen, sobald das Gut zurück ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The warning to watch for the return of the stolen goods and, when they are back, to carry the stone to the place it lay and to loose the bush again."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "A caution about what the operator owes after the effect has occurred, not about the risk of performing it. Both printings and the older booklet carry it in the same order and with the same clause; no consequence of leaving the bush bent is named in any of them."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 65, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The protection of the carried booklet"@en, "Der Schutz des getragenen Büchleins"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim, printed after the charm against powder and ball, that whoever carries this booklet is safe from all his enemies, visible and invisible; that whoever has it with him cannot die without the whole corpus Christi, cannot drown in any water, cannot burn in any fire, and that no unjust sentence can be spoken over him."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim stands word for word in the Romanus-Büchlein of seventeen eighty-eight and again in the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, and in both it says this booklet. The referent therefore moves with the reprinting: the sentence that guarantees the bearer of one book is carried over unchanged into another and guarantees the bearer of that one. Nothing in either printing marks the transfer."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage the transmission is most reliably misreported on. It is regularly given as a Pennsylvania German claim of eighteen twenty; it was in print in Silesia thirty-two years earlier. The title page of the Reading printing does not in fact claim otherwise: it says the greater part is still little known and appears in print in America for the very first time, which is a claim about America and not about first publication. The English title page of eighteen fifty drops in America from the first half of that sentence and thereby makes the wider claim the German never made."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 66, S. 29, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57" .

#
# Added 2026-09-13. The run of 2026-09-05 read five passages at the wording and
# left them in the tracker instead of the holding (prima-materia#519, #520,
# #521, #522, #523). That is the fault SPEC §15 names under Aufnahme geht vor
# Vollständigkeit: an issue does not replace a node the passage already
# carries. All five were read again in this run, in the Reading printing of
# eighteen twenty and the Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty, and the
# Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight was opened beside them because one
# of the five turned out not to belong to Hohman at all.
#

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUngluecklicheTage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The table of unlucky days"@en, "Die Tafel der unglücklichen Tage"@de ;
    skos:definition "A fixed list of about thirty-two days of the year, printed month by month, to which four consequences are attached: whoever is born on one of them is unlucky and suffers poverty; whoever falls ill on one of them seldom regains his health; whoever becomes engaged or marries on one of them comes into great poverty and misery; and on such a day one should not move house, not travel, not trade and not begin a lawsuit."@en ;
    skos:note "The days as the Reading printing of eighteen twenty gives them: January one, two, three, four, six, eleven, twelve; February one, seventeen, eighteen; March fourteen, sixteen; April ten, seventeen, eighteen; May seven, eight; June seventeen; July seventeen, twenty-one; August twenty, twenty-one; September ten, eighteen; October six; November six, ten; December six, eleven, fifteen. The Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight agrees except at July, where it prints seven and twenty-one."@en ;
    skos:note "The book names its own source for this one piece and for no other: it was drawn from a hundred-year almanac coming out of Germany, and, the sentence adds, many believe in it. The author signs that sentence with his name. The table is therefore not his claim but a quotation he marks as such, and the transmission it belongs to is the printed almanac and not the handbook."@en ;
    skos:note "Two clauses stand with the table and are not part of it. On the day of the Annunciation, of Simon and Jude, and of the Apostle St Andrew no blood is to be let; and the signs of the zodiac are to be observed by the course of the month as the common almanac gives them daily. The eighteen fifty translation drops the bloodletting clause and keeps the zodiac one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Unglückliche Tage, wie solche in jedem Monat sich befinden«, im unpaginierten Schlussteil vor dem Register" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa.: gedruckt bey J. B., 1828), S. 69" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 57-58" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKalbZeichen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The calf born under the Virgin does not live a year"@en, "Das im Zeichen der Jungfrau geborene Kalb lebt kein Jahr"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim, printed immediately below the table of unlucky days, that a calf whose cow calves in the sign of the Virgin does not live one year, and that one which falls in the Scorpion dies sooner still; from which the book draws the instruction to wean no calf in those signs, nor in Capricorn or Aquarius, so that they do not easily take the deadly inflammation."@en ;
    skos:note "The most examinable sentence on the page and the one the tracker passed over. It names an animal, a determinable date of birth, a deadline of one year and an outcome that either occurs or does not, and it needs no practitioner, no state and no condition. Where the table of days above it requires a run of human lives to test, this requires a herd book."@en ;
    skos:note "It comes from the same hundred-year almanac as the table: the author's sentence about the almanac stands after both and covers the whole piece."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Unglückliche Tage«, im unpaginierten Schlussteil vor dem Register" ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), S. 70" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 58" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The reversed slip against the falling sickness"@en, "Der rückwärts geschriebene Zettel gegen die fallende Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Writing backwards on a slip of paper the words that all is accomplished, once only and not more; laying the writing in a little scarlet-red cloth and a linen cloth over it; and hanging it on unbeschrauen on the first Friday in the new light, with the linen cloth and the thread unbleached."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ceasing of the falling sickness"@en, "Das Weichen der fallenden Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effect the hung slip is worn for: that the falling sickness leaves the sufferer."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The hung slip takes away the falling sickness"@en, "Der angehängte Zettel nimmt die fallende Krankheit"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The book carries a second remedy for the same affliction, without any such condition, some pages further on; the exclusion below therefore belongs to this prescription and not to the affliction."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitAusschluss a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The remedy holds only for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water"@en, "Das Mittel gilt nur für den, der noch nicht ins Feuer oder Wasser gefallen ist"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The heading states the exclusion as the scope of the prescription: it is a remedy for the falling sickness when one has not yet fallen into fire or water. Outside that case the book claims nothing, and the same heading stands a second time in the index of the Reading printing, so it is not a compositor's slip."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "An exclusion in the heading of a prescription is a different thing from an excuse after a failure. It stands before the application, it is determinable on the sufferer beforehand, and it divides the cases into two groups for which the book claims different things. That is what SPEC §15 asks of a failing condition and it is rare in this holding, which otherwise carries conditions that bite only afterwards."@en ;
    skos:note "Whether the division is prognostic or self-protective is not decided here and cannot be decided from the wording. Falling into fire or water is the mark of a severe and manifest seizure disorder, so the clause may exclude exactly the cases in which the remedy would visibly fail. The book gives no reason, and the alternative is left standing rather than filled in."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicDoctorsAusrede a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What words cannot cure, no doctor can cure"@en, "Was mit Worten nicht zu heilen ist, kann ein Doktor erst recht nicht heilen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rejoinder printed in the preface: that the objection, according to which medical treatment no longer helps after the blessing words have been used in vain, is merely the physicians' excuse, because whatever cannot be cured by words can certainly be cured still less by a doctor."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands in the preface, that is at the place where a book says what it is defending itself against. A holding that takes in the prescriptions and leaves out the defence loses the record of how the transmission handles its own failures, and that record is where a copying chain and a series of trials come apart."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Vorrede zur ersten Ausgabe dieses Büchleins, S. 3-4" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), preface, pp. 3-4" .

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelProbe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Celandine and a mole's heart laid on the sick man's head"@en, "Schelwurzel und Maulwurfsherz auf das Haupt des Kranken gelegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taking the herb that grows at the time when the swallows build their nests or the eagles breed, together with the heart of a mole, and laying both on the head of a sick man."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«; die gedruckte Seitenzahl ist im benutzten Exemplar nicht lesbar, der Abschnitt steht zwischen S. 32 und dem Abschnitt »Ein Mittel, Schmerzen zu nehmen«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20, Abschnitt »Swallow-wort«" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelVorzeichen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Weeping for recovery, glad singing for death"@en, "Weinen für die Genesung, fröhliches Singen für den Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sign the laying on is performed to obtain: if the sick man is to recover he weeps, and if he is to die he sings with a glad voice."@en ;
    skos:note "The sharpest claim in the handbook and the only one in it whose two outcomes exclude each other. It requires no interpretation, it is read off the sick man within the same hour, and any case in which the one who weeps dies or the one who sings recovers tells against it. Most of the book promises an effect and leaves the absence of the effect unaccounted for; this predicts, and a prediction can miss."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelYieldsVorzeichen a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The laying on brings out the sign of the outcome"@en, "Das Auflegen bringt das Vorzeichen des Ausgangs hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelProbe ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelVorzeichen ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The same paragraph attaches two further and quite different effects to the same herb: carried with the mole's heart it overcomes and ends all strife, and boiled and filtered its water is a wash for dim or shining eyes. Only the middle one is a prediction, and it is kept apart from the other two here rather than gathered with them."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20" .

#
# The appendix of eighteen twenty-eight. prima-materia#522 asked whether the
# transfer of the disease to a cow belongs to Hohman or to an American editor,
# and said the answer decides how the passage is entered. It was settled by
# opening the first edition: the Reading printing of eighteen twenty has no
# appendix at all, and ends with the morning prayer and the index. The passage
# enters the transmission with the Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight,
# as a reprint from a named newspaper of that same year.
#

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallUebertragung a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The bed built over the cow stable"@en, "Die Bettstelle über dem Kuhstall"@de ;
    skos:definition "Setting up a bedstead for the sick man over the cow stable and keeping him there by day as well, leaving a good opening in the ceiling of the stable so that the exhalation of the stable passes into the room and is breathed by the sick man, and the exhalation of the sick man is breathed by the cow; not mucking out the stable during the operation, though fresh straw may be laid in, and pouring away the cow's milk as unusable for as long as she still gives any."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cow falls dead to the ground"@en, "Die Kuh fällt todt zur Erde"@de ;
    skos:definition "The stated course of the transfer: the animal draws the whole disease to itself little by little, takes the convulsive attacks, and when the man has entirely lost them the cow falls dead to the ground."@en ;
    skos:note "An outcome and not a description of a state. It happens in a stable, to an animal, before witnesses, and it excludes the case in which the sick man and the cow both stay as they were. That is why the passage is here and not left in the tracker."@en ;
    skos:note "The disease is the falling sickness, not gout. The heading of the German appendix reads Mittel wider die Fallende-Krankheit, and what passes to the cow are the gichterische Anfälle, that is convulsive fits; the Harrisburg translation renders gichterisch as arthritic and so produces a claim about gout that the German does not make. The translation error is recorded here because the English printing is the one usually read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallYieldsUebergang a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The shared air carries the disease over to the cow"@en, "Die geteilte Luft trägt die Krankheit auf die Kuh hinüber"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallUebertragung ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The passage is not Hohman's. The Reading printing of eighteen twenty has no appendix; the piece stands first in the Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight, introduced as a remedy made public in the Lancaster newspapers in the year eighteen twenty-eight and closed with the name of the paper, the German-language Lancaster Adler. The Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty renders that name as Lancaster Eagle, which is how the sheet is usually looked for and why it is usually not found."@en ;
    skos:note "A handbook of charms citing a newspaper as its warrant is itself a finding about the transmission, and the warrant it gives is an appeal to European physicians and to repeated trials rather than to a charm. It is recorded here and not in dcterms:source, where SPEC §10 does not admit it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" .
